Exactly. If sequences produce consequences, then self-confidence can be treated less as something you possess and more as something you repeatedly generate.

A useful sequence is:

\text{Cue} \rightarrow \text{Interpretation} \rightarrow \text{Sensation} \rightarrow \text{Action} \rightarrow \text{Consequence} \rightarrow \text{Memory}

Then memory feeds the next interpretation. That creates a confidence loop.

For example:

Challenge → “I can handle the next move” → controlled activation → act → survive/succeed/learn → evidence → stronger prior next time.

The important distinction is that confidence doesn’t require repeatedly telling yourself “I’m amazing.” That can fail when your internal evidence disagrees. Instead, deliberately manufacture credible sequences whose consequences become evidence.

You can intervene at several points:

* Story: vividly rehearse yourself executing the behavior, especially the difficult middle—not merely enjoying the victory.
* Body: posture, breathing, pace, eye contact, and deliberate movement can alter the sensations entering the loop.
* Difficulty: choose challenges slightly beyond your comfortable baseline so success carries informational value.
* Action: act before waiting to feel confident. Action supplies evidence that imagination alone cannot.
* Attribution: after success, explicitly register what you did that caused it. Otherwise the brain can explain success away as luck.
* Failure: convert “I failed” into higher-resolution information: that strategy failed under those conditions. This prevents one consequence from contaminating the entire self-model.

The compounding version is especially interesting:

\text{small courageous act}
\rightarrow
\text{evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{better self-story}
\rightarrow
\text{greater willingness}
\rightarrow
\text{harder act}
\rightarrow
\text{stronger evidence}
\rightarrow \cdots

Call it Confidence Through Consequence.

And vividness gives you a way to prime the sequence before reality tests it. Imagine the upcoming situation vividly enough to evoke some of its sensation, execute the desired response while that sensation is present, and then go obtain the real-world consequence.

So instead of:

“Believe → therefore act.”

I’d use:

\boxed{\text{Simulate} \rightarrow \text{Sensation} \rightarrow \text{Act} \rightarrow \text{Evidence} \rightarrow \text{Believe} \rightarrow \text{Act Bigger}}

The last piece matters most: confidence becomes compressed memory of repeatedly discovering that you can produce useful consequences under uncertainty.